const footnotes = {
  "1": "Dokku Pro is a paid add-on ($849 lifetime license at time of writing; 1 production + 2 pre-production servers). It adds a web UI, a JSON:API REST API, HTTP(S) git push and team management, and runs on your own server. It is not hosted. [DK14][DK15]",
  "2": "Kamal is installed on your machine (Ruby gem or Docker) and drives servers over SSH. [KM2]",
  "3": "CapRover deploys \"a supported subset of Docker Compose\" from the dashboard. The docs call it \"still experimental\". Fields like `build`, `networks`, `secrets` and `deploy` are ignored. [R3]",
  "4": "Dokku's deployment methods are git push, `git:sync`, archives and images. We found no Compose deployment method in its docs. [DK6][DK17]",
  "5": "Kamal deploys one app image per service. Supporting containers (databases, caches) are \"accessories\" defined in `deploy.yml`. [KM5]",
  "6": "`caprover deploy` from your project directory, plus dashboard upload and webhooks. CapRover's docs also show its HTTP API being scripted for backups. [R5][R6]",
  "7": "Coolify deploys the same image to several standalone servers. You put your own load balancer in front. Compose apps and apps with persistent storage can't use this yet. Docker Swarm support was experimental, is deprecated, and will be removed in Coolify v5 in favor of Compose replicas and Coolify's own scaling. [C6][C7][C8]",
  "8": "Dokku's default scheduler runs on one host. The k3s scheduler adds worker and server nodes and needs 2 GB RAM on every node. [DK2][DK3]",
  "9": "Kamal deploys to every host listed. With more than one server, \"you need to put a load balancer in front of them\". [KM2]",
  "10": "CapRover scales by adding nodes to its Docker Swarm cluster, managed from the main CapRover instance. [R4]",
  "11": "\"The default proxy shipped with Dokku is nginx\". It can be switched with `proxy:set`. [DK4]",
  "12": "Certificates can be imported with the built-in ssl plugin. Automatic Let's Encrypt uses the optional `dokku-letsencrypt` plugin. [DK5]",
  "13": "kamal-proxy's automatic HTTPS \"requires that we are deploying to one server\" with the `host` option set. [KM6]",
  "14": "CapRover auto-deploys by storing repo credentials or an SSH key and giving you a webhook URL. Its docs show GitHub and Bitbucket, and say \"GitLab and Others\" work the same way if they send a POST webhook. There is no OAuth/app-style integration. [R5]",
  "15": "Dokku accepts `git push` from anywhere and can pull from any repo with `git:sync` (plus `git:auth` for private servers). There is no provider-specific integration. [DK6]",
  "16": "Kamal builds the version you have checked out and pushes it through a registry. It doesn't connect to Git hosts. Use your CI to run it on push. [KM2]",
  "17": "Dokploy's docs describe previews as \"specifically designed for applications with GitHub integration\". [D8]",
  "18": "Dokku's official GitHub Action has `review-apps:create` / `review-apps:destroy`. GitHub only. [DK12]",
  "19": "A Coolify rollback redeploys an older image still retained on the server. It doesn't undo database migrations. [C13]",
  "20": "CapRover's rollback rebuilds and deploys the selected version's image. It doesn't revert environment variables or other app config. [R5]",
  "21": "We found no rollback command in Dokku's docs. They mention keeping prior images \"for manual rollback\" by disabling image cleanup. The k3s scheduler has a `rollback-on-failure` setting. [DK7][DK3]",
  "22": "PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, MongoDB, Redis, KeyDB, Dragonfly, ClickHouse. [C1]",
  "23": "MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB, MariaDB, libsql, Redis. [D2]",
  "24": "Official Dokku datastore plugins include Postgres, MySQL, MariaDB, Mongo, Redis and others. [DK10]",
  "25": "Kamal accessories are containers you define (image, host, volumes). They aren't updated on deploy and get no zero-downtime deploys. [KM5]",
  "26": "CapRover's backup covers the CapRover instance \"except images and volumes\". For volumes \"you have to use a custom solution\". The docs call the feature experimental. [R6]",
  "27": "Done with plugin commands. For example, `postgres:backup-auth` sets up S3 credentials, `postgres:backup <service> <bucket>` uploads a backup, and `postgres:backup-schedule` schedules one. [DK11]",
  "28": "Scheduled backups of volume and directory mounts, stored locally or on S3-compatible storage, with retention controls. Added in v4.3.0. [C15]",
  "29": "Docker named volumes only, not bind mounts. Stored on S3 destinations. The Hobby Cloud plan allows 1 volume backup per application. [D10][D18]",
  "30": "363 templates in Coolify's one-click service catalog (`templates/service-templates.json`) on 2026-10-19. Browse them on coolify.io/services. [C16]",
  "31": "Dokploy offers \"open-source templates (Plausible, Pocketbase, Calcom, etc.) with a single click\". We couldn't find a published total. [D2]",
  "32": "CapRover's homepage says \"dozens of popular services\". A community-maintained directory is also available. [R1][R7]",
  "33": "Sentinel reports server and container health. Metrics add CPU and memory history. Threshold alerts and long-term retention need an external tool. [C17]",
  "34": "\"You can enable NetData which is a server monitoring tool\". [R8]",
  "35": "CapRover Pro (paid plans) adds \"build status notifications\", login alerts and two-factor auth. We found no managed hosting offering. [R9]",
  "36": "The community plugin list includes Slack and Telegram notification plugins. [DK10]",
  "37": "Kamal hooks (`pre-deploy`, `post-deploy` and others) run your own scripts, which you can use to send notifications. [KM9]",
  "38": "Kamal's docs show cron running in a dedicated server role whose container runs `cron -f`. [KM8]",
  "39": "`dokku config:set --global` sets variables for all apps. [DK9]",
  "40": "Kamal sets `env` in `deploy.yml`. `.kamal/secrets-common` is shared across destinations. [KM3][KM11]",
  "41": "`kamal deploy -d staging` merges `config/deploy.staging.yml` with the base config. [KM3]",
  "42": "\"Build servers are currently only available for Applications. This feature is not supported for Docker Compose deployments.\" [D14]",
  "43": "CapRover's docs recommend building images in CI (GitHub/GitLab) and deploying the built image to the server. [R10]",
  "44": "Owner, Admin and Member (Member is read-only), applied per team. There are no project-level permissions. [C10]",
  "45": "Free edition: Owner, Admin and Member, where Members get specific permissions (create/delete projects, services, environments, Docker/Traefik/API access and more). Enterprise adds custom roles. [D5][D6]",
  "46": "Team audit log covering UI, API, MCP and webhooks. Events are pruned after 90 days. [C24]",
  "47": "Audit logs are listed as an Enterprise feature. [D6][D18]",
  "48": "Built-in MCP endpoint at `https://<your-coolify>/mcp`. It uses team-scoped API tokens. [C25][C1]",
  "49": "`Dokploy/mcp`, \"The Official MCP package for Dokploy\". [D17]",
  "50": "Community projects exist and are labeled unofficial or early-stage: `ivan-saorin/caprover-mcp` (\"Unofficial CapRover MCP Integration\") and `dokku-mcp/dokku-mcp` (\"not recommended for production use\"). We found no official MCP server on caprover.com, dokku.com or kamal-deploy.org. [X1][X2]",
  "51": "$5/month for 2 servers, +$3/month per extra server. Same features as self-hosted. Apps run on your servers. [C26]",
  "52": "Hobby plan: $4.50/month per server (1 user, 1 organization, 2 environments, some limits). Startup plan from $15/month. Enterprise on request. [D18]",
  "53": "\"Dokku Pro isn't a hosted service.\" [DK14]",
  "54": "Code outside `/proprietary` is Apache-2.0. Code under `/proprietary` is under the Dokploy Source Available License, which requires a commercial agreement for production use. [D19]",
  "55": "CapRover: \"512MB RAM might not be enough\" because builds can use a lot of memory. Most providers offer 1 GB at the low end. CapRover's own comparison page states a 1 GB minimum. [R2][R13]",
  "56": "`kamal audit` shows \"the latest commands that have been run on each server\". [KM13]",
};

